        "Incidence is the measure needed for **aetiological research** and programme evaluation, since only new cases can be attributed to a cause. [Gordis 6e]",
        "**Prevalence** is all cases, old and new, at a given time - it measures the load on services rather than the risk of falling ill. [Park 27e]",
        "**Point prevalence** is at one instant, **period prevalence** over an interval - the second includes those who recovered midway. [Park 27e]",
        "Prevalence is a **proportion, not a rate**, because it has no time in the denominator. [Gordis 6e]",
        "Prevalence is what a **cross-sectional survey** measures, since a single visit cannot observe new cases arising. [Gordis 6e]",
        "Prevalence is the measure needed for **planning services** - beds, drugs, staff. [Park 27e]",
        "For a stable disease **prevalence = incidence x mean duration (P = I x D)** - so anything that lengthens illness raises prevalence. [Park 27e]",
        "Prevalence rises when incidence rises, or when patients live longer without cure - a rise is therefore not proof of more disease. [Park 27e]",
        "Prevalence falls when disease is rapidly fatal, rapidly cured, or cases migrate out, so a fall can mean cure or death alike. [Park 27e]",
        "Insulin raised the **prevalence** of diabetes without changing its incidence, because it lengthened survival with the disease. [Park 27e]",
        "A fatal Ebola epidemic has a **high incidence and a low prevalence**, since cases die before they can accumulate. [Park 27e]",
        "**Cumulative incidence (risk)** uses persons at risk at the start as denominator - a proportion. [Gordis 6e]",
