  redFlags: [
    "**Material that has never been pre-tested on the intended audience.** Printing a lakh of posters after showing the draft only to colleagues is the commonest and costliest error; pre-testing on a sample of the real audience for attraction, comprehension, acceptability, involvement and persuasion is not optional and takes days, not months.",
    "**A message pitched above the audience's literacy or in technical language.** Words such as anaemia, hypertension, screening, sputum conversion and even nutrition may carry no meaning; if the reading level of a pamphlet exceeds that of the people it is meant for, it has communicated nothing and has quietly told them the service is not for them.",
    "**Ignoring the local language, dialect or idiom.** A Hindi banner in a Bhojpuri, Santhali or Gondi-speaking block, or a translation done by a clerk rather than tested with speakers, wastes the budget and marks the campaign as an outside imposition; the words the community itself uses for the illness must be used.",
    "**A fear appeal without an offered, achievable solution.** High threat with low efficacy drives fear control rather than danger control - denial, avoidance, fatalism and anger at the messenger - so a graphic image of a diseased lung or a dying child must always be paired with a specific, feasible, affordable action and a place to go for it.",
    "**No feedback channel and no monitoring.** A one-way campaign that provides no way for the audience to ask, object or report cannot detect that its central image is being misread, that a rumour has taken hold, or that the material never left the district store - and discovers the failure only in the end-line survey.",
    "**Creating demand for a service that is not available, not affordable or of poor quality.** Advertising institutional delivery where the FRU has no blood or no anaesthetist, or free treatment where the drug is stocked out, converts a communication success into a permanent loss of trust and makes the next campaign harder.",
    "**Stigmatising, blaming or coercive language.** Terms such as leper, TB patient as a source of infection, defaulter, non-compliant, mentally retarded, AIDS victim, or messaging that shames women for family size, open defecation or a child's malnutrition, drives concealment and non-attendance; the person is separated from the condition, and the message addresses the barrier rather than the character.",
  ],
  pearls: [
    "Social marketing is the application of commercial marketing concepts and techniques to achieve a **voluntary behaviour change** for the welfare of the individual and of society - the term was coined by **Kotler and Zaltman in 1971** in the Journal of Marketing, from Wiebe's 1951 question of why brotherhood cannot be sold like soap.",
    "Social mobilisation is **bringing together all feasible and practical intersectoral partners and allies** to raise awareness of and demand for a development objective, to assist in the delivery of resources and services, and to strengthen community participation for sustainability and self-reliance - learn this sentence verbatim, it is worth two marks on its own.",
    "The elements of communication are **sender, message, channel, receiver and feedback**, with noise acting throughout. It is feedback that makes it communication rather than announcement, and feedback is the element most often missing from Indian campaigns.",
    "The **seven Cs** of a good message: credibility, content, context, clarity, continuity and consistency, channels, and capability of the audience. Quote all seven and give one health example of each.",
    "The **cultural barrier** is the most important barrier to health communication in India - illiteracy, custom, belief, religion, caste, gender norms and the social distance between a doctor and the family. Working through a trusted local opinion leader dissolves it faster than any amount of repetition.",
    "**Knowledge is necessary but not sufficient.** The ladder runs unaware - aware - concerned - knowledgeable - motivated - trial - sustained practice; mass media can move people up the first two or three rungs, only interpersonal communication reliably moves them up the last three.",
    "In the four Ps, **place and price defeat promotion nearly every time**. Nirodh succeeded because it was in the paan shop at a token price, not because of the advertisement; JSY succeeded because it attacked the cash and transport price of delivery. If a campaign has failed, look first at availability and at the non-monetary price, not at the poster.",
    "**Pre-test, always.** Test with the intended audience for attraction, comprehension, acceptability, involvement and persuasion; a comprehension failure found in a focus group costs a week, and the same failure found after distribution costs the entire budget and a year of credibility.",
    "**Never create demand you cannot meet.** The communication plan and the service-readiness plan must be launched in that order only if supply is already in place; the reverse sequence is the single most reliable way to lose a community's trust.",
    "The **added Ps** - people, partnership, policy and purse strings - are what make a social marketing effort last after the campaign money is gone; universal salt iodisation survives because it was written into law and into the salt trade, not because of any advertisement.",
    "Evaluate at three levels: **process** (what was done - sessions held, spots aired, materials distributed), **outcome** (what changed in knowledge, attitude, practice and service uptake) and **impact** (what changed in coverage, morbidity or mortality). Counting materials distributed and calling it evaluation is the classic examination trap.",
    "In the consultation, **teach-back** is the cheapest quality-assurance tool in medicine: ask the patient to tell you in their own words how they will take the treatment at home. Comprehension failures found in the room are free; the same failure found at the next visit costs a relapse.",
  ],
