      answer: 1,
      explanation:
        "The framework divides climate effects into direct (heat illness, injury and death from extreme weather), ecosystem-mediated (changes acting through natural systems - vector ecology, water and food-borne infection, aeroallergens, crop yield, zoonoses) and socially mediated (displacement, livelihood loss, food insecurity, conflict, mental illness). Vector-borne disease is the classic ecosystem-mediated pathway: warming shortens the extrinsic incubation period of the virus in Aedes aegypti, raises biting frequency and vector survival, and extends the altitude, latitude and season over which transmission is possible. Direct effects are those of the physical exposure itself on the human body. Urbanisation, water storage and waste certainly contribute to Aedes breeding, but that makes it a co-determinant, not an alternative classification, and the observed altitudinal shift tracks temperature. Calling it a mitigation failure confuses a policy category with a causal pathway.",
