  pearls: [
    "**In India the sex ratio is females per 1000 males; almost everywhere else it is males per 100 or 1000 females.** Open the answer with the Indian definition and the examiner knows you have read the Indian text.",
    "**Child sex ratio = girls per 1000 boys aged 0-6 years, and it was 918 in the 2011 census - the lowest since independence** (927 in 2001, 945 in 1991). The overall sex ratio in the same census was 943, its best since 1961. Explain the paradox: adult female survival is improving while fewer girls are being born.",
    "**The natural sex ratio at birth is about 950 girls per 1000 boys.** Any large population persistently below that is selecting, not unlucky.",
    "**The killer mechanism to write down is fertility decline interacting with son preference:** a smaller desired family concentrates the demand for a son into fewer births, which is why the worst ratios are in the richest, most educated, lowest-fertility states and districts, and why the ratio worsens steeply with birth order.",
    "**Kerala (1084) and Puducherry (1037) highest, Haryana (879) and Punjab (895) lowest; for the child sex ratio Haryana 834 and Punjab 846 are the worst** - all 2011 census figures. Always attach the census year to a census number.",
    "**PNDT Act 1994, in force 1 January 1996, amended 14 February 2003 to become the Pre-conception and Pre-natal Diagnostic Techniques (Prohibition of Sex Selection) Act** - the amendment added pre-conception techniques and brought ultrasound and imaging centres explicitly under registration.",
    "**Section 4(3) indications: age above 35, two or more spontaneous abortions or foetal loss, exposure to a teratogenic agent, family history of mental retardation or physical deformity or other genetic disease, and any other condition specified by the Central Supervisory Board.** Five items - a favourite one-line question.",
    "**Form F for every obstetric ultrasound, before the scan, complete and signed, preserved for two years from the last entry, returns by the fifth of the next month.** The prosecution almost always begins with the paperwork, not with proof of disclosure.",
    "**Penalties: doctor 3 years and Rs 10,000 for a first offence, 5 years and Rs 50,000 for a subsequent one, with removal from the medical register for 5 years and then permanently; the husband or relative who seeks sex selection faces the same imprisonment but fines of Rs 50,000 and Rs 1,00,000.** Offences are cognizable, non-bailable and non-compoundable.",
    "**The Act prohibits sex selection, not abortion.** Lawful termination is governed by the MTP Act 1971 as amended in 2021. Refusing a woman a legal abortion because of PCPNDT is both wrong in law and dangerous in practice - she will find an unsafe one.",
  ],
